  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 212 PM EDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\n- Rain possible Saturday night into Sunday, mainly north\n\nGenerally light rain will be possible as early as Saturday night \nthanks to mid/lower tropospheric isentropic ascent and moist \nadvection. There will be a deep and dry residual boundary layer this \nprecipitation will be falling through, resulting in substantial sub-\ncloud evaporation that will limit accumulations. this is expected to \npersist into the day Sunday. Rainfall accumulations currently look \nto be limited to north of I-96.\n\n\n- Lesser thunderstorm concerns early next week\n\nAt this time yesterday, we were concerned about a heavy rain threat \nSunday night. Now we are seeing in the ensemble guidance a distinct \nnorthward shift of the precipitation focus, more towards northern \nLower MI, and with lesser precipitation amounts overall. Over Lower \nMI, it appears that instability will be questionable although we \nwill see a gradual deepening of atmospheric moisture by early\nMonday.\n\n\n- Unseasonably hot and humid Monday and Tuesday\n\nThe concern for most impactful weather has pivoted from heavy rain \nto oppressive heat. Heat indices exceeding 90 degrees both Monday \nand Tuesday afternoons look highly probable (greater than 80 percent \nchance). While this is not that exceptional when compared to what \noccurs during a typical summer, it will feel manifestly different \ncompared to the dry and seasonably cool weather we've had lately. \nAdditionally, this coincides with the start of the school year for \nmost locations, so there could be vulnerability for those \nfacilities without air conditioning. The greatest threat will be \ntowards I-94 where dewpoints could make a serious run at 70F, \nwhich would compound the oppressiveness.\n\n\n- Uncertain timing for thunderstorms the entire coming week\n\nCapping will limit afternoon thunderstorm potential at least \nearly in the week. As noted previously, better chances for showers\nand storms will occur at night when a nocturnal low-level jet can\nbecome established and contribute to both conditional destabilization\n(via low level theta-e advection) and forcing for ascent (via warm\nadvection, used as a proxy to the Laplacian of thickness advection).\n\nThe magnitude and placement of processes like these, being inherently\nelevated, are difficult for NWP to model. Moreover, synoptic flow\nand associated forcing can be significantly altered by the latent\nheat release effects of previous convection. Thus, predictability\ndecreases rapidly with increasing forecast projection times. The \nmedium range deterministic models certainly bear this out with \nvery different solutions. However, it is safe to say based on \nlatest ensemble guidance that chances for showers and thunderstorms\nwill persist at least through Friday.",
